{
 "case": "N03-SECRET-NEG",
 "says": "Alice's laptop reads its fresh profile (active, generation 1); she binds her device key and registers a private notes source, then locks the profile (generation 2). She tries to unlock while her key's private half sits beside the public key the signing call names: the unlock is refused with private_key_beside_public and the profile stays locked, because under that layout OpenSSH would prompt for the passphrase again outside the unlock. She tries again while SSH_AUTH_SOCK names a signing agent this runtime does not own: refused with agent_not_owned_here. While locked, the signed commit of her notes' first revision is not admitted, with access_locked: no signature is made. With the public half stored apart and the runtime's own agent, an unlock at a terminal succeeds with the real device key (still generation 2: an unlock moves no generation), and the same commit is admitted. What must not happen: a signature made while locked, an unlock through an agent the runtime does not own, or any passphrase prompt other than the one inside the unlock transition.",
